<p>Britain raised its national terrorism threat level from “substantial” to “severe” after two Jewish men were stabbed on April 29 in Golders Green, north London, and police declared the attack a terrorist incident. “Severe” is the second-highest level on the five-point scale and means intelligence agencies consider an attack highly likely within the next six months. The Metropolitan Police said on May 1 that it had charged Essa Suleiman, 45, with two counts of attempted murder and one count of possessing a bladed article in a public place. The government said Britain was facing an antisemitism emergency, and Prime Minister Keir Starmer pledged more police in Jewish areas, tougher action against antisemitic incitement and legislation aimed at state-sponsored threats, including from Iran. Detectives were trying to determine a motive and whether there was any link to Iranian proxies.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/a6OKMfPX_J3Jq_M65rGz_te7OwMjCyPeQV1Vtvm03qiJSoNuyZrnf3DUgQSImtrdMfXO_L-J14V9pDQvEO0Ox8ZqH46Gbn20Nz3uXL7veZXYt3mFjIy3qwE0aVc7MRwHpzT88uN-PhBqe2_6vBCb1ytz7zTuI6R1A5A8metpNcxPpJlTiSsikFP8zXF_SDwKNqZ0t1IyNpC798Ubv2PWh5WC' alt='A crime scene investigator at the site of a stabbing attack in the Golders Green neighborhood of London on Wednesday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Victims’ condition: The two victims, aged 76 and 34, were seriously injured but were reported to be in stable condition in hospital.</li><li>Threat rationale: Officials linked the raised threat level to a broader risk environment, including Islamist and extreme right-wing terrorism, rather than the Golders Green attack alone.</li><li>Suspect history: Police said the suspect had a history of serious violence and mental health issues, and sources reported he had previously been referred to the Prevent counter-extremism programme.</li><li>Earlier incident: Detectives also examined a separate altercation earlier on April 29 in southeast London in which a person received minor injuries.</li><li>European concern: European counterterrorism officials expressed concern after a series of attacks on Jewish-linked buildings and property, while U.S. and European officials examined whether similar incidents suggested low-cost hybrid warfare tactics.</li></ul><blockquote>People are scared, scared to show who they are, scared to go to synagogue, scared to send their children to school as Jews - Keir Starmer</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Keir Starmer: About 100 protesters heckled Starmer during a visit to Golders Green, where some community members accused the government of failing to protect them. (<a href='https://www.koaa.com/world/europe/uk-raises-national-terror-threat-level-after-the-stabbing-of-2-jewish-men'>KOAA</a>)</li><li>Jonathan Hall KC: The government’s independent reviewer of terrorism and state threats legislation told the BBC that attacks on Jewish people in the UK now posed the biggest national security emergency since 2017. (<a href='https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/antisemitism-national-security-emergency_uk_69f3094ee4b0f004aa7824ef'>HuffPost UK</a>)</li><li>Jewish residents in London: A local resident told CBS News that warnings about rising antisemitism had long gone unheeded, comparing smaller incidents before the stabbing to waves before a tsunami. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/london-antisemitic-attacks-jewish-residents-describe-horror-fear/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Kemi Badenoch: The Conservative leader pointed to a decade-long escalation in antisemitic incidents and pressed ministers to respond to anti-Jewish extremism after the stabbing. (<a href='https://nyheder.tv2.dk/udland/2026-04-30-trusselsniveauet-haeves-efter-terrorangreb-i-london'>TV2</a>, <a href='https://www.thejc.com/news/uk/antisemitism-a-national-emergency-says-badenoch-after-golders-green-stabbing-in8tkkwm'>The JC</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/uk-raises-terror-threat-level-following-antisemitic-attack